   "line": [
    "Bg5"
   ],
   "spoken": "The Queen's Gambit Declined is the ideal beginner's opening — a gateway into a whole world of classical play. The traditional developing move is the bishop to e7, but there's a sharper try for a little extra edge, one that looks for all the world like it hangs a pawn."
  },
  {
   "ply": 8,
   "t": 97,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pppn1ppp/4pn2/3p2B1/2PP4/2N5/PP2PPPP/R2QKBNR w KQkq - 4 5",
   "line": [
    "Nbd7"
   ],
   "spoken": "That try is the knight to d7 — and the point is a trap plenty of players walk straight into. It makes a perfectly sound theoretical move while quietly laying a snare.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 9,
   "t": 99.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pppn1ppp/4pn2/3p2B1/2PP4/2N1P3/PP3PPP/R2QKBNR b KQkq - 0 5",
   "line": [
    "e3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 8,
   "t": 101,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pppn1ppp/4pn2/3p2B1/2PP4/2N5/PP2PPPP/R2QKBNR w KQkq - 4 5",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 9,
   "t": 119,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pppn1ppp/4pn2/3p2B1/2PP4/2N1P3/PP3PPP/R2QKBNR b KQkq - 0 5",
   "line": [
    "e3"
   ],
   "spoken": "e3 is the main line, and now the bishop develops to e7 rather than d6 — because there's a pin to break. Answering the pin and freeing the position is the priority."
  },
  {
   "ply": 10,
   "t": 132,
   "fen": "r1bqk2r/pppnbppp/4pn2/3p2B1/2PP4/2N1P3/PP3PPP/R2QKBNR w KQkq - 1 6",
   "line": [
    "Be7"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 11,
   "t": 135,
   "fen": "r1bqk2r/pppnbppp/4pn2/3p2B1/2PP4/2N1PN2/PP3PPP/R2QKB1R b KQkq - 2 6",
   "line": [
    "Nf3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 12,
   "t": 140,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/pppnbppp/4pn2/3p2B1/2PP4/2N1PN2/PP3PPP/R2QKB1R w KQ - 3 7",
   "line": [
    "O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": "Round out the kingside by castling short — get the king safe before committing the rest of the army."
  },
  {
   "ply": 13,
   "t": 147,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/pppnbppp/4pn2/3p2B1/2PP4/2N1PN2/PP3PPP/2RQKB1R b K - 4 7",
   "line": [
    "Rc1"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 14,
   "t": 186.5,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/pp1nbppp/2p1pn2/3p2B1/2PP4/2N1PN2/PP3PPP/2RQKB1R w K - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "c6"
   ],
   "spoken": "Here's the offbeat gem — the simplifying system. Instead of the usual queenside fianchetto, play the modest c6. Be warned, it looks bad: passive, and it does hem in the light-squared bishop. But there's a method behind the passivity.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 15,
   "t": 195,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/pp1nbppp/2p1pn2/3p2B1/2PP4/2NBPN2/PP3PPP/2RQK2R b K - 1 8",
   "line": [
    "Bd3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 207,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/pp1nbppp/2p1pn2/6B1/2pP4/2NBPN2/PP3PPP/2RQK2R w K - 0 9",
   "line": [
    "dxc4"
   ],
   "spoken": "Now the paradox pays off — capture on c4. Because the exchange was delayed until the opponent's light bishop had already committed, recapturing it costs them a tempo. Patience turns into a free move."
  },
  {
   "ply": 13,
   "t": 209,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/pppnbppp/4pn2/3p2B1/2PP4/2N1PN2/PP3PPP/2RQKB1R b K - 4 7",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 210.5,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/pp1nbppp/2p1pn2/6B1/2BP4/2N1PN2/PP3PPP/2RQK2R w K - 0 9",
   "line": [
    "dxc4",
    "Bxc4",
    "c6"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 15,
   "t": 212.5,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/pppnbppp/4pn2/6B1/2BP4/2N1PN2/PP3PPP/2RQK2R b K - 0 8",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 215.5,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/pp1nbppp/2p1pn2/6B1/2BP4/2N1PN2/PP3PPP/2RQK2R w K - 0 9",
   "line": [
    "c6"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 20,
   "t": 274.5,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp1nqppp/2p1p3/3n4/2BP4/2N1PN2/PP3PPP/2RQK2R w K - 0 11",
   "line": [
    "Bh4",
    "Nd5",
    "Bxe7",
    "Qxe7"
   ],
   "spoken": "The heart of the system is the knight to d5, offering to trade the dark-squared bishops and recapturing with the queen so the knight sits on a genuine stronghold. It's a marvellous teacher of how pieces and pawns really work — at first glance the position looks worse, with a passive bishop and less of the centre, yet it's close to dead equal.",
   "kind": "plan",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 21,
   "t": 286.5,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp1nqppp/2p1p3/3n4/2BP4/2N1PN2/PP3PPP/2RQ1RK1 b - - 1 11",
   "line": [
    "O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": "The reason it holds is the complete absence of weaknesses — an under-appreciated truth of chess. A position with no targets is extremely hard to crack, no matter how passive it looks. Solidity itself is a resource."
  },
  {
   "ply": 22,
   "t": 326,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp1nqppp/2p1p3/8/2BP4/2n1PN2/PP3PPP/2RQ1RK1 w - - 0 12",
   "line": [
    "Nxc3"
   ],
   "spoken": "Firing e5 at once would misfire, because the knight would be left loose on its central post. So capture on c3 first, and only then uncoil with e5 — expanding on the right terms and opening the bishop's diagonal in the process.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 24,
   "t": 332.5,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp1nqppp/2p5/4p3/2BP4/2R1PN2/PP3PPP/3Q1RK1 w - - 0 13",
   "line": [
    "Rxc3",
    "e5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 25,
   "t": 345.5,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp1nqppp/2p5/4P3/2B5/2R1PN2/PP3PPP/3Q1RK1 b - - 0 13",
   "line": [
    "dxe5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 26,
   "t": 346.5,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp2qppp/2p5/4n3/2B5/2R1PN2/PP3PPP/3Q1RK1 w - - 0 14",
   "line": [
    "Nxe5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 28,
   "t": 350.5,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp3ppp/2p5/4q3/2B5/2R1P3/PP3PPP/3Q1RK1 w - - 0 15",
   "line": [
    "Nxe5",
    "Qxe5"
   ],
   "spoken": "Equality is reached; the last job is to free the light-squared bishop, and e6 is the square, neutralising the opponent's bishop. A queen sortie to b3 would try to discourage it, and a kingside pawn thrust holds no fear — the position is simply too solid, with nothing to attack."
  },
  {
   "ply": 32,
   "t": 431.5,
   "fen": "r4rk1/pp3ppp/2p1B3/4q3/8/2R1P3/PPb2PPP/5RK1 w - - 0 17",
   "line": [
    "Qc2",
    "Bf5",
    "Be6",
    "Bxc2"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 36,
   "t": 434,
   "fen": "r4rk1/pp3ppp/2p1q3/8/8/2R1P3/PPb2PPP/5RK1 w - - 0 19",
   "line": [
    "Bd7",
    "Qd6",
    "Be6",
    "Qxe6"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 37,
   "t": 454,
   "fen": "r4rk1/pp3ppp/2p1q3/8/8/2R1P3/PPb2PPP/3R2K1 b - - 1 19",
   "line": [
    "Rd1"
   ],
   "spoken": "A rook lifts to the d-file, and a loose pawn appears — but before ever snatching such a pawn, run the check that the queen isn't walking into a trap. That verification is never optional."
  },
  {
   "ply": 38,
   "t": 484,
   "fen": "r4rk1/pp3ppp/2p5/8/8/2R1P3/qPb2PPP/3R2K1 w - - 0 20",
   "line": [
    "Qxa2"
   ],
   "spoken": "The pawn can be taken safely — even if the rook lunges to harass, the queen simply retraces its steps to e6, the very square it came from, with the extra pawn pocketed.",
   "kind": "tactic"
  },
  {
   "ply": 39,
   "t": 487,
   "fen": "r4rk1/pp3ppp/2p5/8/8/2R1P2P/qPb2PP1/3R2K1 b - - 0 20",
   "line": [
    "h3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 40,
   "t": 528.5,
   "fen": "r4rk1/pp3ppp/2p1q3/8/8/2R1P2P/1Pb2PP1/3R2K1 w - - 1 21",
   "line": [
    "Qe6"
   ],
   "spoken": "The queenside pawns demand real care here — natural-looking tries run into a rook fork of the queen and the a-pawn. The clean answer is just to shuttle the queen back to e6 and sidestep all the tricks."
  },
  {
   "ply": 41,
   "t": 536.5,
   "fen": "r4rk1/pp3ppp/2p1q3/8/8/3RP2P/1Pb2PP1/3R2K1 b - - 2 21",
   "line": [
    "Rcd3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 42,
   "t": 580,
   "fen": "r3r1k1/pp3ppp/2p1q3/8/8/3RP2P/1Pb2PP1/3R2K1 w - - 3 22",
   "line": [
    "Rfe8"
   ],
   "spoken": "Against a rook trying to burrow into the seventh rank, there's a technique worth committing to memory: play the rook to e8 first, so that the infiltration to d7 can be met by the other rook stepping to e7 — a forcing offer to trade off the invader.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 43,
   "t": 587.5,
   "fen": "r3r1k1/pp1R1ppp/2p1q3/8/8/4P2P/1Pb2PP1/3R2K1 b - - 4 22",
   "line": [
    "Rd7"
   ],
   "spoken": "And the detail that makes it work: if the rook shifts along the back rank instead, it's simply captured, and the defending rook drops back to e8 unharmed. The invasion is neutralised either way.",
   "kind": "tactic"
  },
  {
   "ply": 45,
   "t": 598,
   "fen": "r5k1/pp2rppp/2pRq3/8/8/4P2P/1Pb2PP1/3R2K1 b - - 6 23",
